  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Saturday...\n\nWeak shortwave moving thru eastern MT early this morning is dry, \nbut an associated Pacific cold front produced modestly gusty winds\nalong the western foothills (gusts 30-40 mph) last evening, and \nwill shift winds to westerly across our east this morning. There \nis a small window for patchy fog before the wind shift, and some \nlow stratus may eke into southeast MT as well, but otherwise the \nwind shift will scour out any low level moisture and end the risk \nof fog/low clouds. Downslope gradients in place will keep SW-W \nwinds breezy along the western foothills today into tonight. \nExpect gusts up to ~40 mph with an outside chance (25%) of \ntouching 50 mph. Otherwise, warm and dry conditions continue today\nwith high temps in the 50s to lower 60s. Temps on Monday will be \ncomparable to today but with less wind along the foothills. For \nthose keeping score, the first two weeks of November ran nearly \n10F warmer than normal across the region.\n\nCooling should begin Tuesday with height falls and a shift to NE-E\nwinds. A Pacific wave will be kicked SW to NE thru the area \nbringing a good (albeit brief) chance of precip Tuesday night into\nWednesday. Mountains will see a period of accumulating snow (2-5\"\nover the western mountains, with a 20% chance of reaching 6\"). \nFoothills and perhaps higher hills east of the foothills may see a\nbit of wet snow, but overall this will be a seasonably warm \nsystem with snow levels not going below 4-4.5kft. Thus most lower \nelevations will see at most a period of rain showers. Expect \nprecip amounts of 0.20-0.45\" in the west and 0.05-0.15\" elsewhere,\nthough there remains uncertainty with the strength of forcing and\npotential splitting so this is subject to change. South central \nand southeast parts will likely receive the least, given the \nexpected track of the wave.\n\nWednesday through the end of the week will see cooler (i.e. more\nseasonable) temperatures. Wednesday night & Thursday look dry as\nwe await a stronger low off the Pacific coast, a feature that will\nmove through the intermountain west in some fashion Thursday night\nto Saturday. Must stress high uncertainty in details but today's\nmodel runs have overall trended drier for our forecast area.\nCuster analyses favor either an unorganized open wave or a deeper\nupper low that tracks too far south. Ensembles show high\nconfidence in a strong ridge in NW Canada and this would seem to\nfavor a slower evolution than the progressive wave favored by the\nGFS and its ensembles, but it's hard to say. To bring our region \nmeaningful precip and lower elevation snow, the low would need to \nevolve through WY bringing upslope flow and allow for colder \nCanadian air to advect southward at the same time...a solution \nthat current cluster analyses show as least likely. Putting that \nall aside, until we become more certain, look for temps in the 30s\n& 40s with a 30-50% chance of rain & snow Thursday night to \nSaturday. JKL",
